## Why This Is Asked
Tests multi-cloud, multi-env orchestration, and robust gating. It probes backend scoping, provider aliasing, and policy-as-code integration in a realistic production-like flow.

## Key Concepts
- Per-env Terraform backends and state isolation
- Provider aliasing for multi-cloud deployments
- Policy-as-code gates (Sentinel/OPA) for prod safety
- Wrapper modules for consistent tagging across clouds

## Code Example
```javascript
// backend.tf (Terraform S3 backend per environment)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "org-terraform"
    key    = "state/${var.environment}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Providers with aliases (multi-cloud)
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
  alias  = "aws"
}
provider "google" {
  project = var.gcp_project
  region  = var.gcp_region
  alias   = "gcp"
}
```

```javascript
// Wrapper module usage for tags
module "shared_tags" {
  source      = "./modules/tags"
  environment = var.environment
  cost_center = var.cost_center
}
```

```javascript
// Simple OPA/rego style policy (example)
package terraform.policy
default allow = false
allow {
  input.environment != "prod"
}
```

```javascript
// CI gate steps (high level)
terraform init
terraform plan -out=tfplan
terraform show -json tfplan > plan.json
# Evaluate plan.json against policy (opa or sentinel)
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a cloud-agnostic Terraform module with clean separation of concerns, provider aliasing, and robust gating to prevent drift or accidental exposure.

## Key Concepts

- Provider aliases and multi-cloud orchestration
- Module abstraction over provider-specific resources
- Per-environment backends and key management
- CI plan gating with JSON plan validation
- Basic test coverage via Terratest for existence and config

## Code Example

```terraform
# Root usage
variable "cloud" { type = string }
variable "bucket_name" { type = string }
variable "versioning" { type = bool }
variable "public_read" { type = bool }
variable "environment" { type = string }

provider "aws" { alias = "aws" region = "us-east-1" }
provider "google" { alias = "google" project = var.environment }
provider "azurerm" { alias = "azurerm" features = {} }

module "object_store" {
  source = "./modules/object_store"

  cloud       = var.cloud
  bucket_name = var.bucket_name
  versioning  = var.versioning
  public_read = var.public_read
  environment = var.environment
}
```

```terraform
# modules/object_store/main.tf (skeleton)
variable "cloud" { type = string }
variable "bucket_name" { type = string }
variable "versioning" { type = bool }
variable "public_read" { type = bool }
variable "environment" { type = string }

provider "aws" { alias = "aws" }
provider "google" { alias = "google" }
provider "azurerm" { alias = "azurerm" }

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  count = var.cloud == "aws" ? 1 : 0
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  acl    = var.public_read ? "public-read" : "private"

  versioning {
    enabled = var.versioning
  }
}

# google and azurerm blocks would mirror the above with appropriate attributes
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to support policy-driven controls (e.g., CFRs) across clouds?
- How would you test drift between environments and prevent accidental exposure in prod?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a GitOps, multi-cloud per-PR sandbox architecture with isolated backends, secret management, and policy gates. It also probes automation for ephemeral environments and teardown reliability.

## Key Concepts
- Per-PR sandbox architecture across AWS, Azure, GCP
- Provider aliases per cloud and per PR
- Per-PR remote backends and state isolation
- Vault-backed credentials with rotation
- Encryption at rest and in transit
- Policy-as-code gates (OPA/Sentinel) in CI
- Teardown automation on PR lifecycle

## Code Example
```hcl
# Minimal scaffolding
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = { source = "hashicorp/aws" }
    azurerm = { source = "hashicorp/azurerm" }
    google = { source = "hashicorp/google" }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "pr1"
  region = "us-east-1"
}
provider "azurerm" {
  alias    = "pr1"
  features = {}
}
provider "google" {
  alias   = "pr1"
  project = "pr-12345"
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement per-PR teardown if resources are tainted or fail during apply?
- What strategies ensure Vault rotation never disrupts in-flight deployments while keeping state secure?
